The Safe Way to Recode (this was used as the above code gave error)
Don't use the simple NR % 95 awk trick from before — that can cause collisions. Use this instead, which assigns clean sequential numbers:
bash# First, get the list of unique chromosome names in order

cut -f1 pca_data.bim | sort -u > chrom_names.txt

# Check how many chromosomes you have
wc -l chrom_names.txt

// 21 chrom_names.txt- there are 21 chromosomes 

Then recode:
## Create a chromosome name mapping file ##

awk '{print $0, NR}' chrom_names.txt > chrom_map.txt

# Recode the .bim file using the mapping
awk 'NR==FNR {map[$1]=$2; next} {$1=map[$1]; print}' \
    chrom_map.txt pca_data.bim > my_data_admix.bim

# Copy the other files unchanged
cp pca_data.bed my_data_admix.bed
cp pca_data.fam my_data_admix.fam
// my_data_admix.bed my_data_admix.fam -these 2 files get created

## Verify the Recoding Worked ##
// Should show numbers like 1, 2, 3 ... instead of CM063088.1 etc.
cut -f1 my_data_admix.bim | sort -u

######################################running admixture with 5 fold cross validation ######################

conda activate admixture

for K in $(seq 1 5); do
    admixture --cv=5 -j8 my_data_admix.bed $K | tee log_K${K}.txt
done

################# Step 3: Extract Cross-Validation Errors #################
## Collect CV errors from all log files ##

grep "CV error" log_K*.txt | awk '{print $3, $4}' | sed 's/(K=//;s/)://' | sort -k1,1n > cv_errors.txt

